    "role": "assistant",
    "type": "assistant_message",
    "content": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded — coverage is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\n**16 PASS / 3 FAIL**\n\nCore task management is solid: creation with all fields, editing, deletion, completion, active/completed views with accurate counts, both sort modes (priority and deadline, with undated tasks last), empty/whitespace title rejection, blocked-task distinction and warnings, cycle prevention (both direct and indirect dependents were correctly excluded from the prerequisite list), and the prerequisite-warning hover tooltip.\n\n**Three failures:**\n\n- **FT-3 — Blocked tasks are entirely non-editable.** The requirement is that a blocked task keeps its description and priority editable while title and deadline lock. In reality the edit button is fully `disabled`, the form never opens, and the card exposes no inline editor — only delete remains enabled. The \"title/deadline unchangeable\" half holds only incidentally, because nothing at all can be changed.\n\n- **CS-22 — Invalid completed-and-blocked state, unrecoverable.** With both \"Complete tax filing\" and its prerequisite \"Review project proposal\" completed, reopening the prerequisite was permitted and left the dependent sitting in the *completed* view while simultaneously showing `Prerequisite \"Review project proposal\" incomplete`. The system neither blocked the reopen nor reset the dependent. Worse, the dependent's toggle is now disabled, so the user cannot reopen it to escape the contradictory state.\n\n- **IX-18 — Connector hover is unreachable and its popup never renders.** The dashed connections and their tooltip triggers are built correctly (16px transparent hit paths, correct chain text in the DOM), but two defects stack: the overlay SVG has `pointer-events: none` at `z-0` behind the task cards, so across 120 sampled points per connector a real mouse never hits the line; and even when the trigger is fired directly, the popup lays out at 0×0 with opacity 0 — whereas the warning tooltip on the same page rendered visibly at 248×70. So the dependency chain is never visibly presented via the connection.\n\nA methodological note on IX-18: an intermediate measurement suggested both tooltips were 0×0, but that turned out to be a stale stuck-open tooltip artifact. After reloading for clean state, the warning tooltip rendered correctly under a real mouse hover while the connector tooltip still did not — which is what separates the IX-20 pass from the IX-18 failure."
  }
}
